  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Safeguarding Amur tiger conservation, through strengthening the effectiveness and funding of UALZZT protected area</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">There are around 500 individual Amur tigers today. The Zoological Society of London (ZSL) has been working in Lazovsky Zapovednik State Nature Reserve, and Zov Tigra National Park, using camera traps to monitor tiger density, reproduction, survival, and distribution. These two protected areas merged to form the United Administrations of Lazovsky Zapovednik and Zov Tigra National Park (UALZZT). ZSL and NGO partners have strengthened anti-poaching and law enforcement, through the SMART (Spatial Monitoring and Reporting Tool) approach and the Forest Eyes Initiative (enhanced surveillance through camera trap technology). Project objectives are to, (1) Enhance the long-term tiger monitoring program in UALZZT by maintaining and upgrading the camera trap grid and expanding the grid to an additional 500km2; (2) Improve the long-term poacher cam surveillance Forest Eyes Initiative system to sustain and enhance anti-poaching in UALZZT by comparing and evaluating systems and identifying vulnerable gaps in monitoring; and, (3) Strengthen capacity for effective and sustainable protected area management by enabling partnerships between tiger range states, supporting a knowledge exchange visit and partnership between two tiger range states, Russia and Nepal.</narrative>
  </description>
